ate crude lysates  of viral rna  that would then be paired or used  together with inhibitor tolerant pcr  chemistries or direct to pcr  or rt pcr chemistries and both of these  have resulted in time  savings but when we're doing an analysis  of looking at what the long arm of this  process are  amplification clearly stands out as one  of the things that doesn't seem to have  been greatly impacted  are in our response to pandemic testing  and so the question is are there any  methods that are available for  decreasing this sort of  70 to you know two and a half hour  window of amplification  well there's uh certainly literature  evidence that suggests  that uh this can be done there is  additionally  a point of care device uh type of  methodologies  uh that can do single sample  acceleration down into sort of the 15  minute window  but those are you know poorly suited for  dealing with thousands if not  millions of samples that need to be  tested  when we look at the literature though um  there have been publications for  instance shown here on the left where  there was a  three step or a three temperature pcr  that underwent 30 cycle  30 cycles of amplification in less than  two minutes  um that technology was deployed by a  group of dutch researchers  in a paper that was published earlier  this year the journal of microbiological  methods  published a paper called fast  identification of e coli and urinary  tract infections  using next generation pcr  and what i think is notable about this  publication is that  what we can see is that their total a  workflow time  they listed as 52 minutes but it  included also dna extraction  pcr electrophoresis then the staining  and de-staining of those gels and then  finally the visualization of that gel  under uv light so  with that in mind if we broke out just  the number that they said for the  cycling it was a 16 minute protocol so  it suggests that there's methods  available for accelerating  thermocycling by next generati