ottom are yellow and  it's going to shift between them it'll  be in red  as it goes through each of these pairs  of blocks the white and yellow  are set to a specific enter either  denaturation  annealing or extension the plates of the  blocks will come together and compress  the plate  and then subsequently compress the wells  that are within each plate  and so i'm going to go ahead and start  the video now you're going to watch  again the plate come in from the left  and cycle through and then at the  completion of the video i'm going to  walk you through  the what the ramifications of  compressing the plate area  okay  okay  so as we saw in the video we can  eliminate the ramp rates by  using the action of moving the plate  between three temperature zones  but i also refer to the compression of  the plate  by the blocks as another way to  accelerate pcr  the plates themselves are a rigid frame  that you can see here shown on the left  hand side that then have  a ultra thin plastic sheet that then is  laid over top of them where the wells  are then formed into that sheet  the my the ultra thin plastic is about  50 microns thick  and the wells are then laid out in a  standard sps format in either 96 or 384  well formats and then can be  various well volumes after a reaction is  then dispensed  into the well then there's a optical  ready or an optically clear  film that isn't heat sealed on top of  the plate before it's placed into the  machine  when the with this in mind then when  those blocks compress  these wells actually uh deform and the  liquid inside's been shown to mix and  therefore  when the two very large thermal masses  of the blocks compress this plate  squeeze the liquid thin the transfer of  heat is not just  uh to the outside of the well but it's  throughout the entire reaction mixture  and it changes the temperature almost  instantly therefore there's not the need  for these long dwell periods of  for instance 30 seconds or a minute at  particular steps  and we can draw those down to something  that's more like two seconds  five seconds 10 seconds in order to  decrease the protocol  further we went ahead and paired that  together  then with um and deployed that to a  research group at the university of  bielefeld in germany  led by christian call schmidt who then  accumulated some results that were  published earlier this year in med  archives  in a paper titled ultra fast one step  rtpcr  protocol for the detection of sars kobe  2.  what you can see below the title there  is a picture of one of those 9612 plates  that then has been imaged on a  fluorescent imaging system  like a gel documentation system that  then clearly picked up  a fluorescein signal and then you can  see the green and red outlines in those  wells indicating positive and negative  samples  based off of the threshold overall the  paper was able to find a couple of key  findings  they took w-h-o published  assays and accelerated them in 96 well  plates  with protocols including the reverse  transcription step  that resulted in a 16 minute protocol  if you look at all three assays that  were tested within the paper  they had a 96.6 sensitivity with  remnant clinical samples that were used  at one-fifth the recommended volume  suggesting a very sensitive detection  method  now this methodology is a single target  assay um that highlights an effective  screening protocol that could be used  and in the image that i showed here on  the left it's with one of the uscdc  design  assays with an fda cleared master mix  that has been uh that was used in the  paper  so overall the procedure looked  something like this in the paper where  they  selected the plates in this case they  selected a 96 well plate that had a 20  microliter reaction volume of course  they come in different plate sizes and  reaction numbers but they're all sps  format and can be used with liquid  handlers  they then use the optically clear films  that i alluded to earlier to seal it in  a heat sealer  using an anvil to then create a seal  around each  reaction chamber so there's no  cross-contamination  after putting into the thermocycler for  16 minutes that accomplished the reverse  transcription  as well as the cycling they took the  plate  flipped it over and put it onto a blue  led light imager for fluorescent  detection  after capturing that image a simple jpeg  file  they then used a software package to  analyze the fluorescent data and convert  it into  uh raw fluorescent units that were then  able to be put into for instance  an expert with the analysis of a simple  table and so what you can see here is  then a table that corresponds to the  wells  uh where their fluorescent values were  captured for each of the different wells  this data then could be converted into  an analysis software you could look at  threshold values you could deliver it to  a for instance a laboratory information  system  they continued this and for instance one  of the programs that they  accelerated was the shards hay protocol  that was published  earlier this year with an assay  targeting starscope v2  using the quantum bio q script xlt  rt master mix and they did a nice  dilution series  here with the synthetic rna supplied by  twist bioscience  and you can see in the figure on the  left hand  side there's sort of a decrease in  amounts of copies per reaction  with the top row being the no template  control representing the background of  the reaction  and then above the plate you can see the  cycling conditions where  there was an rt step followed by uh  then a heating activation of the rt  reaction and then a 40 cycle protocol  and so as they went through this they  were then able to uh  then create a cutoff by  taking the average fluorescent value  across the no template control  adding three times the standard  deviation to create a threshold value so  they could call positive and negative  samples  the same approach then you know the  question becomes what is the analytical  sensitivity of a method like this  and in one of the examples um they've  gone ahead and  put together a figure that's shown here  on the left where they looked at the  analytical sensitivity  of the china cbc developed assays  against  orf1ab as well as the n gene  and by using a dilution series and then  seeing where it crossed the threshold  value  you can see a really great analytical  sensitivity of  about two and a half and about four and  a half copies per microliter  the same result the same type of curves  were also done for the uscdc assays the  duros and charity assays as well as  acids from the institute pasteur  so this kind of provides some uh  support for using uh the assay for as a  sensitive methodology but  what would it look like for instance in  practice  and so now uh in collaboration with  groups  in suriname and molecular biology  systems  deployed three units um there are three  machines  and they've done a large uh study with  clinical  narrative geoswabs that were collected  and first tested with a roche light  cycler or a g  expert to a den and to identify their  identities and samples and what you can  see then  is on the right hand side a scatter plot  that then  compares the relative fluorescent units  into assay from the uscdc on next-gen  pcr  as it relates to the ct value that was  derived from the n assay  on a roche light cycler and what i've  drawn in the red lines there for you is  uh the one that's  um crosses right around 50 58  60 relative fluorescence units above  that was considered  positive by next-gen pcr uh and  fluorescent detection  and then the vertical line represents  the ct threshold cutoff that they're  using for this assay of 38  and so that you can see then if you  analyze the deposit percent agreement  there was 116 out of 121 samples were  correctly  identified and there was a 100 negative  percent agreement over 350 samples  suggesting that this protocol is  something that could b