rol  so when we take the this study together  you know some of the key findings are  that the  fluorescent play reader was clearly able  to directly read the next generation pcr  microplates next generation pcr  successfully cycled a multiplex assay  three and a half times faster  than the protocols that are currently in  use  and there is a belief by the team that  there's a further optimization of the  cycling protocol  could accelerate the assay even further  and then lastly when you look at the  dilution series and the wide range of  fluorescent values there's obviously  significant dynamic range  that then could deal with either  very very high viral loads are very low  viral lows and then in summary of what  we've shown you today  um you know when we think about the  context of how much testing do we need  there have been estimates in the united  states that we need somewhere between  200 000 and a million tests be  performed per hour in order to  successfully reach some of the estimates  of what's required  and if we look historically up to just a  few days ago the  absolute peak rate of testing was only  at about 49  50 000 samples per hour so that's less  than 25  of the stated goal by multiple uh  organizations and so if we think about  that um  you know and the impact of that  obviously the earlier statistics around  the 40 percent of tests arrive too late  to be clinically useful  um is a result of the fact that current  systems can really only perform about  132 to 250 tests per hour  and so the results that we've shared  with the community today are really  about what next generation pcr system  a single unit system could perform about  1100  test results without pooling could be  performed on a 384 well plate  um their estimates between four and  eight times faster than the current  systems  um the system which actually is shown  over my shoulder there  it only takes up about the size of two  shoe boxes  in total and they use the exact same  fda clear